# Overview

Semi-targeted peptidomics of endogenous amidated peptides across a mouse tissue
panel (N = 3 per tissue). Peptide identity is confirmed by MS/MS spectral
matching against authentic synthetic standards, using dot product similarity,
precursor mass accuracy and fragment ion count.

Detection is reported as a binary call per replicate. No absolute or relative
concentrations are derived: the calibration strategy does not support absolute
quantitation, so this analysis reports only whether spectral identity was
confirmed.

Accompanies Wiggenhorn *et al.*, "Global profiling of peptide amidation to
discover bioactive fragments of the secretome."

### Input

A single file: **Supplemental Table 2**, as published with the paper.

| Sheet | Supplies |
|---|---|
| `Ion Integrations` | Skyline peak areas for the 1 µM synthetic standard and every tissue replicate |
| `spectral valid` | precursor mass accuracy per replicate; also used to verify the recomputation |
| `Detected Peptides` | peptide naming for figure labels |

Everything below is recomputed from those areas. Running this document
reproduces the published detection calls exactly — the checks in sections 4 and
6 will fail loudly otherwise.

**Note on fingerprint ions.** The published `Ion Integrations` sheet contains
the precursor plus the fragment ions used for scoring, so this document takes
the fingerprint as published and recomputes the scores from it. The selection
rule is stated in section 3; re-deriving the fingerprint from scratch requires
the full Skyline transition export, which contains all monitored ions.

---

# 1. Configuration

```{r config}
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)
library(readxl)
library(gplots)

PATH_TABLE_S2 <- "data/Supplemental_Table_2.xlsx"
OUT_DIR       <- "results"
dir.create(OUT_DIR, showWarnings = FALSE, recursive = TRUE)

# ── Detection criteria (Methods) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY   <- 0.85  # primary threshold
DOT_PRODUCT_SECONDARY <- 0.70  # relaxed threshold, once a peptide is confirmed
PPM_THRESHOLD         <- 15    # precursor mass accuracy (ppm)
ION_COUNT_PRIMARY     <- 3     # fingerprint ions, primary criterion
ION_COUNT_SECONDARY   <- 2     # fingerprint ions, relaxed criterion
DP_DIGITS             <- 2     # dot products are reported, and criteria
                               # applied, at 2 dp — matching the replicate-to-
                               # replicate reproducibility of the measure
                               # (median within-tissue SD ~= 0.13)

# Peptides where the standard ion requirement cannot be met for physical
# reasons. TRH is a tripeptide: a1, b1 and y1 fall below the m/z 120
# acquisition floor and y2 gives no signal in the synthetic standard, leaving
# only b2 and a2 observable.
MIN_IONS_OVERRIDE <- c("TRH" = 2)

TISSUE_ORDER <- c("BAT", "Brain", "Ileum", "Heart", "Kidney", "Liver",
                  "Lung", "Pancreas", "Quad", "Spleen", "iWAT", "plasma")
```

---

# 2. Read Supplemental Table 2

```{r read}
# Peak areas. Header on row 1.
integrations <- read_excel(PATH_TABLE_S2, sheet = "Ion Integrations") %>%
  transmute(
    peptide   = `PEP Name`,
    is_std    = grepl("^Standard", `Sample Type`),
    replicate = Replicate,
    tissue    = Tissue,
    ion       = `Fragment Ion`,
    area      = ifelse(is.na(as.numeric(Area)), 0, as.numeric(Area))
  )

# Precursor mass accuracy, and the published values used for verification.
# Header on row 2, hence skip = 1.
published <- read_excel(PATH_TABLE_S2, sheet = "spectral valid", skip = 1) %>%
  transmute(
    peptide   = `PEP Name`,
    tissue    = Tissue,
    replicate = Replicate,
    ppm       = as.numeric(`Precursor Accuracy (ppm)`),
    pub_dp    = as.numeric(`Dot Product`),
    pub_ions  = as.integer(`Ion Count`),
    pub_call  = `Is Detected` == "Yes"
  )

cat("Integration rows:", nrow(integrations),
    "| replicate records:", nrow(published),
    "| peptides:", n_distinct(integrations$peptide), "\n")
```

---

# 3. Fingerprint ions

Each peptide is scored on a fixed set of fragment ions taken from its synthetic
standard. Those ions were chosen as the most intense qualifying ions in the
1 µM standard, each required to sit more than 0.5 Da — the fragment matching
tolerance — from every ion already selected, so no measured peak contributes
twice to a score.

Selection uses the standard alone and happens **before any tissue data are
examined**. This is what makes a high dot product meaningful: choosing ions
after inspecting tissue data would make the criterion uninformative, since a
large fraction of arbitrary 3–4 ion combinations can be made to pass for almost
any replicate.

```{r fingerprint}
standard_areas <- integrations %>%
  filter(is_std, ion != "precursor") %>%
  select(peptide, ion, std_area = area)

fingerprint <- standard_areas %>%
  group_by(peptide) %>%
  summarise(ions = paste(ion, collapse = ", "),
            n_ions = n(), .groups = "drop")

cat("Peptides:", nrow(fingerprint),
    "| ions per peptide:", paste(sort(unique(fingerprint$n_ions)), collapse = ", "), "\n")

stopifnot(all(standard_areas$std_area > 0))   # no dead channels in a fingerprint
```

---

# 4. Dot product

$$\text{dot product} = \frac{\sum A_i S_i}{\sqrt{\sum A_i^2}\;\sqrt{\sum S_i^2}}$$

$A_i$ = peak area in the sample, $S_i$ = peak area in the standard, summed over
the fingerprint. Both vectors describe the same channels, so the score reflects
agreement in relative intensity.

```{r dot_product}
scores <- integrations %>%
  filter(!is_std, ion != "precursor") %>%
  inner_join(standard_areas, by = c("peptide", "ion")) %>%
  group_by(peptide, tissue, replicate) %>%
  summarise(
    ion_count   = sum(area > 0),
    dot_product = round(sum(area * std_area) /
                        (sqrt(sum(area^2)) * sqrt(sum(std_area^2))), DP_DIGITS),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

# Verify against the published values before going further
check <- scores %>% inner_join(published, by = c("peptide", "tissue", "replicate"))
stopifnot(nrow(check) == nrow(published))
cat("dot products reproduced:", sum(check$dot_product == check$pub_dp |
                                    (is.na(check$dot_product) & is.na(check$pub_dp))),
    "/", nrow(check), "\n")
cat("ion counts reproduced  :", sum(check$ion_count == check$pub_ions),
    "/", nrow(check), "\n")
```

---

# 5. Detection criteria

Two tiers. A peptide is **confirmed** if any replicate reaches the primary
criterion; once confirmed, further replicates of that peptide may qualify under
the relaxed criterion. A peptide never confirmed has no detections at all.

- **Primary:** dot product ≥ `r DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY`, |ppm| < `r PPM_THRESHOLD`, ≥ `r ION_COUNT_PRIMARY` ions
- **Relaxed:** confirmed peptide, dot product ≥ `r DOT_PRODUCT_SECONDARY`, |ppm| < `r PPM_THRESHOLD`, ≥ `r ION_COUNT_SECONDARY` ions

```{r detection}
results <- scores %>%
  left_join(select(published, peptide, tissue, replicate, ppm, pub_call),
            by = c("peptide", "tissue", "replicate")) %>%
  mutate(
    min_ions = ifelse(peptide %in% names(MIN_IONS_OVERRIDE),
                      MIN_IONS_OVERRIDE[peptide], ION_COUNT_PRIMARY),
    ppm_ok   = !is.na(ppm) & abs(ppm) < PPM_THRESHOLD
  ) %>%
  group_by(peptide) %>%
  mutate(
    confirmed = any(!is.na(dot_product) & dot_product >= DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY &
                    ppm_ok & ion_count >= min_ions),
    is_real_detection = confirmed & !is.na(dot_product) & ppm_ok &
      ((dot_product >= DOT_PRODUCT_PRIMARY   & ion_count >= min_ions) |
       (dot_product >= DOT_PRODUCT_SECONDARY &
        ion_count >= pmax(ION_COUNT_SECONDARY, min_ions - 1)))
  ) %>%
  ungroup() %>%
  select(-min_ions, -ppm_ok)

cat("detection calls reproduced:",
    sum(results$is_real_detection == results$pub_call), "/", nrow(results), "\n")
```

---

# 6. Final panel

```{r panel}
detected <- results %>% filter(is_real_detection)

cat("peptides:",    n_distinct(detected$peptide),
    "| detections:", nrow(detected), "\n")

# Fail loudly if the inputs or criteria drift
stopifnot(
  all(results$is_real_detection == results$pub_call),
  n_distinct(detected$peptide) == 62,
  nrow(detected) == 787
)
```

